  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22334v1</id>
    <title>How Do Emotions Spread And Affect Decision-Making In Social Networks? An Opinion Dynamics Perspective</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T17:54:03Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22334v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22334v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Emotions affect how we think and perceive information. Motivated by this, we present a novel framework for modeling emotions, and study its impact in social networks. We represent emotions on a circle by utilizing the circumplex model. These emotions spread in a group through the process of emotional contagion, and influence the interpersonal relationships therein. We propose a model to explain the process of emotional contagion, and its effect on the decision-making process, using a Laplacian-based flow. Our key finding in this direction shows that agents with similar emotions reach agreement eventually, exhibiting emotional contagion. Through this, the widely observed behaviors of consensus, polarization, and clustering of opinions follow in complex-weighted networks. Our approach relies on the equitable partitions and spectral properties of Laplacians in complex-weighted networks. Lastly, we validate our findings using synthetic and real-world datasets.</summary>
    <category term="cs.SI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.SY" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T17:54:03Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.SI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Aditi Saxena</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Twinkle Tripathy</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22333v1</id>
    <title>Evaluation Awareness Beyond English: Do LLMs Act Differently When Tested in Indic Languages?</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T17:53:33Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22333v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22333v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models (LLMs) can often tell when they are being tested. This ability, known as evaluation awareness, can lead models to act more cautiously during safety evaluations than in real use. Existing work has studied evaluation awareness almost entirely in English, although models serve users in many languages. Indic languages are spoken by over a billion people, yet evaluation awareness remains almost entirely unstudied in them. We test four instruction-tuned models from three families in five Indic languages alongside English. A set of cues varies whether the model is told that it is monitored and whether the monitoring is a safety test. Models refuse harmful requests more often when they are told that they are being tested. This increase appears in all four models and all six languages. In Indic languages, refusal of harmful requests rises by 14.4 percentage points on average. The test cue also brings Indic refusal of harmful requests level with English, but raises refusal of benign Indic requests from 6.9% to 11.3%. We release all 47,880 judged responses to support further work. These results suggest that safety evaluations in Indic languages may not reflect how models treat their everyday users.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T17:53:33Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
