  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22320v1</id>
    <title>Not Just a Subject: Capturing Adaptive Scene-Specific Variance for Visual Storytelling</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T17:51:49Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22320v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22320v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recent advancements in diffusion models have significantly improved text-to-image generation, enabling their application to visual storytelling, where coherent image sequences are generated from narrative prompts. However, existing methods primarily focus on identity preservation and often fail to adapt to diverse scene variations, resulting in semantic misalignment and structural inflexibility. We propose EOS-Story, a training-free framework that harmonizes identity consistency with scene-specific adaptation across text and vision spaces. In the text space, we decouple the narrative into identity and scene sub-prompts to prevent information dilution, but this leaves the scene without access to appearance cues. To bridge this gap, we introduce Cross-Contextual Bridging, which softly injects a phrase-level appearance residual into the scene's &lt;EOS&gt; embedding. In the vision space, we find that scale mismatch between identity and scene subjects causes a few identity tokens to be repeatedly transferred, producing structural artifacts. We address this with Adaptive Identity Preservation, which anchors identity features at the scene-specific scale (Identity Anchoring) and integrates them via Identity-Aware Self-Attention. To evaluate performance under varied spatial compositions, we extend the Consistory+ benchmark with diverse framing scenarios (Consistory+_+). Extensive experiments show that EOS-Story achieves competitive or superior performance on both benchmarks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T17:51:49Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted at ACCV 2026. Code: https://github.com/SeungJuCha/EOS-Story</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>SeungJu Cha</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ye-Chan Kim</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kwanyoung Lee</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Dong-Jin Kim</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22319v1</id>
    <title>A team of Gemini 4 Argon agents beats Minecraft</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T17:51:32Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22319v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22319v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We placed four Gemini 4 Argon agents in a sandboxed Minecraft survival world and gave them one goal: defeat the Ender Dragon. Each agent controlled a player via an open-source library called Mineflayer and coordinated with its teammates through in-game chat and shared memory. No one assigned roles or wrote game logic. The agents planned, wrote, and debugged every program themselves. Researchers could watch the run on a live dashboard and pause or stop it at any time, but they had no cause to intervene. Starting from a randomly generated world on Easy difficulty, the team wrote 2,783 programs and defeated the dragon in 27 hours and 14 minutes, dying 605 times along the way. To our knowledge, this is the first end-to-end autonomous completion of Minecraft by AI agents, achieved using only the standard Mineflayer library without pre-scouted seeds or other game-specific tools written by humans. Winning required the agents to keep a coherent plan across thousands of dependent steps for more than a day. Complex real-world software engineering and enterprise tasks demand the same long-horizon coding and multi-agent coordination. We describe the setup, how the run unfolded, the team's strategies (dividing the work, sharing what they learned, recovering each other's items), and the limits of the result.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T17:51:32Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Maxence Faldor</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Satinder Singh</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Roberta Raileanu</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22316v1</id>
    <title>Learning to Steer LLM Agents and Multi-Agent Systems</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T17:51:17Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22316v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22316v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language model (LLM)-based agents and multi-agent systems increasingly tackle complex tasks through long-horizon tool use and inter-agent collaboration. However, these systems are prone to deviating from the overall goal as execution unfolds, which wastes execution resources and leads to undesired outcomes, a failure termed goal drift. In this work, we propose a steering agent that supervises execution on the fly and steers the system back on track when drift occurs. The steering agent observes the execution context, deciding when to intervene with a low-cost fast path and how to intervene with a high-cost slow path. To unlock its steering intelligence, the ability to assess goal drift and generate effective interventions, we jointly train the two paths with reinforcement learning, combining a pairwise ranking objective with group-relative policy optimization (GRPO). Experiments on information-seeking search tasks show that the steering agent provides effective supervision for both single-agent and multi-agent systems, improving their performance and efficiency. The learned steering intelligence generalizes to out-of-domain tasks, transfers to unseen executors, and supports controllable intervention frequency without retraining.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T17:51:17Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>21 pages, 6 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Leyang Shen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yaxin Gong</name>
    </author>
